  "narrative": "I assessed all new discovery evidence using the signed convention and approximate 10%-of-absolute-mean clinical scales: EGFR about 0.025, ERBB2 about 0.026, KRAS about 0.0245, KIF18A about 0.026, and POLR2A about 0.0255. For direction -1, positive estimates mean stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1, positive estimates mean weaker dependency.\n\nThe PTEN-loss stronger EGFR-dependency program remains the key unresolved candidate. H112 broadened high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype without restricting to adenocarcinoma: R78 is +0.0293 with interval -0.0060 to +0.0645. The point estimate exceeds the EGFR clinical scale, but the interval crosses null and its lower bound is below threshold, so it supports but does not establish a clinically meaningful broad high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype association. H108 adds specificity against PIK3CA-mutant confounding: R81 is +0.0400 with interval -0.0023 to +0.0822. That is encouraging because the effect remains after restricting to PIK3CA-wildtype high-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma, but it still does not exclude the null and remains contextually selected. H105 remains the strongest precise claim, R74 = +0.0435 with lower bound +0.0047, and it is selected for independent validation. This validation request is motivated by the need to address multiplicity: the PTEN EGFR signal has emerged through several overlapping contexts, including H96, H105, H108, H112, H107, and H114. Independent validation of H105 will test whether the best selected context replicates or whether it is a selection artifact.\n\nH107 tests the lower-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma subgroup: R79 is +0.0354 but with a very wide interval, -0.0375 to +0.1083, and small cells, 42 exposed and 192 comparator. The point estimate is compatible with the PTEN EGFR signal, but uncertainty is too large to draw a conclusion, and low-Cas9 missing Cas9 activity may introduce selection/missingness effects. I defer this exact low-Cas9 claim. H114 tests whether PTEN EGFR dependency is enriched in high-Cas9 versus lower-Cas9 KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma: R80 is +0.0129 with interval -0.0692 to +0.0950. This does not support a Cas9-activity interaction, so I defer the interpretation that the signal is specifically a high-Cas9 artifact. However, an imprecise null interaction also does not fully exclude Cas9-dependent attenuation.\n\nThe specificity controls are mostly reassuring but incomplete. H113 tests PTEN-loss associated with stronger ERBB2 dependency in the same clean context: R82 is -0.0043 with interval -0.0454 to +0.0367. The point estimate is near zero and opposite the clinically meaningful direction, but because the upper bound exceeds the ERBB2 clinical threshold, I cannot rule out a clinically meaningful positive ERBB2 effect. Thus ERBB2-specificity remains unresolved and deferred. H109 tests PTEN-associated weaker KRAS dependency in the same context: R83 is -0.0127 with interval -0.0498 to +0.0245. Because the direction is +1, negative signed values indicate the opposite of weaker dependency, and the upper bound is at or just below the KRAS clinical scale. I reject the exact PTEN-loss weaker KRAS dependency claim. This supports the idea that PTEN loss is not broadly weakening RAS-pathway dependency in this context, although the data cannot exclude clinically meaningful stronger KRAS dependency, which was not the registered claim. H110 tests PTEN-associated stronger POLR2A dependency: R84 is +0.0024 with interval -0.0394 to +0.0442. The point is near null, but the interval remains wide enough to include clinically meaningful values, so this core-transcription control is unresolved and deferred rather than conclusive.\n\nFor KIF18A, the high-Cas9 TP53-loss refinement does not sharpen the signal: H115 R85 is +0.0200 with interval -0.0047 to +0.0446, below the KIF18A threshold and not excluding null. H116 tests RB1 loss as a new mitotic predictor: R86 is +0.0082 with interval -0.0193 to +0.0357, near null. Neither is clinically supported, so both exact claims are unresolved and deferred. The broader H33 TP53-loss KIF18A signal remains unresolved but less attractive after H115.\n\nThe POLR2A specificity controls provide helpful negative-control information for accepted headline predictors. H122 tests SMARCA4 loss with stronger POLR2A dependency: R87 is -0.0050 with interval -0.0241 to +0.0141; the upper bound is below the POLR2A clinical scale, so I reject the exact stronger-POLR2A claim. This supports specificity of the SMARCA4 SMARCA2 signal. H123 tests KEAP1 loss with stronger POLR2A dependency: R88 is -0.0071 with interval -0.0269 to +0.0126, also below threshold, supporting specificity of the KEAP1 NFE2L2 signal. H124 tests TP53 loss with stronger POLR2A dependency: R89 is +0.0140 with interval -0.0057 to +0.0338. The point is below threshold and near null, but the upper bound exceeds the clinical scale, so this control remains unresolved and deferred.\n\nOverall, these results do not establish new clinically meaningful effects. They refine the PTEN-loss EGFR signal as plausible but context-sensitive and potentially selection-sensitive, while giving partial reassurance against global POLR2A dependence and PTEN-associated weaker KRAS dependency. No new predictor or subgroup signal clears the clinical threshold with acceptable uncertainty. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 7 appraise update. PTEN-loss EGFR remains primary unresolved candidate. H105 selected for independent validation because it is the strongest precise context: KRAS-wildtype adenocarcinoma high-Cas9, R74 +0.0435 lower +0.0047. Supportive but incomplete context evidence: H96 broader no-Cas9 +0.0305 lower -0.0023; H112 broad high-Cas9 KRAS-wt +0.0293 lower -0.0060; H108 PIK3CA-wt high-Cas9 adeno +0.0400 lower -0.0023; H107 low-Cas9 +0.0354 wide; H114 Cas9 interaction +0.0129 wide null. Specificity: H109 PTEN weaker KRAS rejected because upper bound at/below KRAS threshold and point opposite; H113 ERBB2 not supported but CI includes clinically meaningful stronger ERBB2; H110 POLR2A near null but wide. KIF18A: H115 TP53 high-Cas9 not sharpened; H116 RB1 not promising. POLR2A controls: SMARCA4 and KEAP1 reject stronger POLR2A, supporting specificity; TP53 control near null but interval includes meaningful values, deferred. Next: use H105 validation to decide PTEN EGFR; if validation fails, reject selected context and downgrade PTEN program. If validation succeeds, accept H105 as supplied-dataset association and cautiously assess broader H96/H112/H108. Remaining untested useful claims include H111 KRAS-mutant high-Cas9 PTEN control, accepted-signal completeness H117-H121, and STAT3 SMAD4 H125. All interpretations are dataset associations, not causality or external generalizability."
